eseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=eeca4d563f65f6d12746bf053e600b1a42ab4305&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; THOMPSON’S GROUP F IS NOT ALMOST CONVEX ... the length of a word in F and upon an analysis of the generators’ geometric ... arXiv:math/0204249v1  [math.GR]  19 Apr 2002\nTHOMPSON’S GROUP F IS NOT ALMOST CONVEX\nSEAN CLEARY AND JENNIFER TABACK\nAbstract. We show that Thompson’s group F does not satisfy Cannon’s al-\nmost convexity condition AC(n) for any integer n in the standard ﬁnite two\ngenerator presentation. To accomplish this, we construct a family of pairs of\nelements at distance n from the identity and distance 2 from each other, which\nare not connected by a path lying inside the n-ball of length less than k for in-\ncreasingly large k. Our techniques rely upon Fordham’s method for calculating\nthe length of a word in F and upon an analysis of the generators’ geometric\nactions on the tree pair diagrams representing elements of F.\n1. Introduction\nCannon [7] introduced the notion of almost convexity for a group G with respect\nto a ﬁnite generating set X. This ﬁnite generating set X determines a word metric\ndX for G and its Cayley graph. G is almost convex (k) or AC(k) with respect to X\nif there is a number N(k) so that for all positive integers n, given two elements y\nand z in the ball B(n) of radius n with dX(y, z) ≤k, there is a path γ from y to z\nof length at most N(k) which lies entirely in B(n). Cannon showed that if a group\nG is AC(2) with respect to a ﬁnite generating set then G is AC(k) for k ≥2 and\nthus a group satisfying AC(2) is called almost convex. Almost convexity allows\nalgorithmic construction of B(n + 1) from B(n) by making it suﬃcient to consider\nonly a ﬁnite set of possible ways that an element in B(n+ 1) can be obtained from\ndiﬀerent elements of B(n).\narXiv:math/0204249v1  [math.GR]  19 Apr 2002\nA number of families of groups have been shown to be almost convex. Cannon [7]\nshowed that hyperbolic groups are almost convex and that amalgamated products\nof almost convex groups are almost convex. Stein and Shapiro [13] showed that\nfundamental groups of closed three manifolds whose geometry is not modelled\non Sol are almost convex. Other families of groups have been shown not to be\nalmost convex. Cannon, Floyd, Grayson and Thurston [5] showed that fundamental\ngroups of manifolds with Sol geometry [5] are not almost convex, and Miller and\nShapiro showed that the solvable Baumslag-Solitar groups BS(1, n) [12] are not\nThe ﬁrst author acknowledges support from PSC-CUNY grant #63438-0032.\nThe second author would like to thank the University of Utah for their hospitality during the\nwriting of this paper.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThompson's Group F - A New Generating Set (https://d31kydh6n6r5j5.cloudfront.net/uploads/sites/66/2019/04/stonesifer_slides_2.pdf)\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.1 years ago; Conjecture: F is not almost convex with respect to Z₁ ... Thompson’s Group F: A New Generating Set\nApplications\n\n1. Dead End with respect to Z₁\n\n2. Conjecture: F is not almost convex with respect to Z₁\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nTop diagram: a horizontal line of black dots with several curved arcs above and below connecting selected dots; two central crossings are labeled “1/2” above and below the crossing point. Smaller arc groups appear on the left and right ends.\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nBottom-left diagram: a horizontal row of black dots with multiple nested arcs rising from the leftmost dot to successive dots to the right; an ellipsis of dots appears in the middle; “1/2” is labeled near the left start and again below a central point, with a small downward arc near the right end.\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e3\">\nBottom-right diagram: similar horizontal dot row with nested arcs from the leftmost dot to successive dots to the right; an ellipsis of dots appears in the middle; “1/2” is labeled near the left start and again below a point near the right end, with a small downward arc near the far right.\n</visual_element>\n\nAlexis Johnson and Amelia Stonesifer  \nThompson’s Group F: A New Generating Set--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn subgroups of R. Thompson’s group F (https://pure.mpg.de/rest/items/item_3118321/component/file_3118323/content)\nciteturn2search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.8 years ago; Gili Golan, Mark Sapir∗ ... Thompson group F and leads to an explicit ﬁnitely generated example. ... arXiv:1508.00493v3  [math.GR]  12 Jan 2017 ... The group F is one of the most mysterious objects in group theory. For example, almost\nOn subgroups of R. Thompson’s group F\nGili Golan, Mark Sapir∗\nJanuary 13, 2017\nAbstract\nWe provide two ways to show that the R. Thompson group F has maximal subgroups of\ninﬁnite index which do not ﬁx any number in the unit interval under the natural action of F\non (0, 1), thus solving a problem by D. Savchuk. The ﬁrst way employs Jones’ subgroup of\nthe R. Thompson group F and leads to an explicit ﬁnitely generated example. The second\nway employs directed 2-complexes and 2-dimensional analogs of Stallings’ core graphs, and\ngives many implicit examples. We also show that F has a decreasing sequence of ﬁnitely\ngenerated subgroups F > H1 > H2 > ... such that ∩Hi = {1} and for every i there exist\nonly ﬁnitely many subgroups of F containing Hi.\n1\nIntroduction\n1.1\nThe stories about F\narXiv:1508.00493v3  [math.GR]  12 Jan 2017\nRecall that the R. Thompson group F consists of all piecewise linear increasing homeomor-\nphisms of the unit interval [0, 1] with a ﬁnite number of linear segments with slopes of the\nform 2n, n ∈Z, and endpoints of the form\na\n2n , a, n ∈N∪{0}. The group F has a presentation\nwith two generators and two deﬁning relations [6, 17] (see below).\nThe group F is one of the most mysterious objects in group theory. For example, almost\nevery 6 months a new “proof” of amenability or non-amenability of F appears. The reason\nwhy all these proofs are wrong is that F is very counter-intuitive. Statements which are\n“obviously true” turn out to be wrong.\nThis can also be illustrated by the story of ﬁnding the Dehn function of F. First it\nwas declared exponential because a sequence of loops in the Cayley graph of F was found\nwith exponential ﬁllings, and it was “obviously impossible” to ﬁnd ﬁllings of these loops with\nsmaller area. Then it was proved to be subexponential and the most probable conjecture (for\nthe same reason as before) was that it is something like 2log2 n. Then a polynomial of degree\n5 upper bound was found and it was conjectured that this bound is optimal. Then a few\nlower bounds were found until Guba proved that F has quadratic Dehn function, the smallest\n∗The research was partially supported by the NSF grant DMS-1500180. The paper was written while the\nsecond author was visiting the Max Planck Institute for Mathematics in Bonn.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJones Representations of Thompson’s Group F Arising from (https://arts.units.it/retrieve/b550d311-b9ef-4634-b41b-cfbb8281100f/21-Aiello-B-Conti-IMRN-Post_print.pdf)\nciteturn2search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Consider the involution σ : t →1 −t of [0, 1] and its associated group isomorphism ... Golan and Sapir proved that the commensurator CommF(⃗F) is equal to ⃗F [17,\nCorollary 3] (and thus ⃗F is not almost normal in F).\na,−a i.e., when a = b or a = −b\nJones showed that the representation π is unitarily equivalent to the quasi-\nregular representation λF/⃗F : F ↷ℓ2(F/⃗F) implying that ˜π and λF/σF(⃗F) are unitarily\nequivalent.\nConsider two subgroups A, B < G. They are called commensurable if A ∩B has\nfinite index both in A and in B. Moreover, they are called quasi-conjugate if there exists\ng ∈G such that A and gBg−1 are commensurable. Finally, recall that the commensurator\nCommG(A) is a subgroup of G equal to the set of g ∈G such that A ∩gAg−1 has finite\nindex both in A and in gAg−1. The following theorem is due to Mackey, see [27] or [10].\nTheorem 4.1.\nIf CommG(A)\n=\nA and CommG(B)\n=\nB, then the quasi-regular\nrepresentations λG/A and λG/B are unitarily equivalent if and only if A and B are\nquasi-conjugate.\nGolan and Sapir proved that the commensurator CommF(⃗F) is equal to ⃗F [17,\nCorollary 3] (and thus ⃗F is not almost normal in F). This implies that CommF(σF(⃗F)) =\nσF(⃗F) as well. Therefore, the representations π and ˜π are unitarily equivalent if and only\nif ⃗F and σF(⃗F) are quasi-conjugate in F.\nWe recall a characterization of ⃗F due to Golan and Sapir [17]. For any dyadic\nrational t ∈(0, 1) there exists a unique n ≥0 and a1, · · · , an ∈{0, 1} such that\nt = \u000bn\ni=1\nai\n2i +\n1\n2n+1 . We write t = .a1 · · · an1. Define S as the set of dyadic rationals\nt = .a1 · · · an1 such that the set of 1 ≤i ≤n satisfying ai = 1 is even.\nTheorem 4.2.\n[17, Theorem 2] The subgroup ⃗F is the stabilizer of S for the usual action\nF ↷[0, 1], i.e., ⃗F = {g ∈F : gS = S}.\n25--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Thompson's group F$F$ is almost 32$\\frac{3}{2}$‐generated (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/370239199_Thompson%27s_group_FF_is_almost_32frac32-generated)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.6 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; 2156 GOLAN-POLAK ... proof that 𝐻 contains the derived subgroup of 𝐹 is almost identical to the proof in part (1) (note\n𝑛 be the branches of 𝑇 and note that there exists 5⩽𝑘⩽𝑛−5 such that the\n\nbranch 𝑢 𝑘≡𝑤0. We use two copies of the tree 𝑇 to construct a new tree diagram (𝑅+,𝑅\n\n−)\n\nin 𝐹 by performing the operations (1𝑎)−(3𝑎) on the first copy of 𝑇 and (1𝑏)−(5𝑏)on the\n\nsecond copy of 𝑇, as in part (1) (but where the trees 𝑇 3 and 𝑇 4 are as defined in the previous\n\nparagraph). By construction, the tree diagram (𝑅+,𝑅\n\n−)has the sets (A) and (B) of pairs of\n\nbranches as in the proof of part (1) as well as the following set:\n\n(𝐶′)\n\n⎧\n\n⎪\n\n⎪\n\n⎪\n\n⎪\n\n⎨\n\n⎪\n\n⎪\n\n⎪\n\n⎪\n\n⎩\n\n𝑤11 → 𝑤110\n\n𝑢 𝑘+3 → 𝑤111\n\n𝑢 𝑖→𝑢\n\n𝑖−1 for 𝑘+4⩽𝑖⩽𝑛−1\n\n𝑢 𝑛 00 → 𝑢 𝑛−1\n\n𝑢 𝑛 01 → 𝑢 𝑛 0\n\n𝑢 𝑛 1→𝑢\n\n𝑛 1.\n\n2156 GOLAN-POLAK\n\nLet g∈𝐹 be the element represented by (𝑅+,𝑅\n\n−). Then 𝜋 𝑎𝑏(g)=(𝑐,0).Let 𝐻=⟨𝑓, g⟩.The\n\nproof that 𝐻 contains the derived subgroup of 𝐹 is almost identical to the proof in part (1) (note\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Golan_G in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AGolan_G+in%3Amath)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; @misc{2607.10961, author = {Gili Golan}, title = {{I}rreducible fast sets of bump homeomorphisms generate copies of {T}hompson's groups ${F}_n$}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2607.10961}, note = {arXiv:2607.10961v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... We prove that Thompson's group $F$ has a subgroup $H$ such that the conjugacy problem in $H$ is undecidable and the membership problem in $H$ is easily decidable.\n\n21 results for au:Golan_G in:math\n\nShow all abstracts\n\n  * Irreducible fast sets of bump homeomorphisms generate copies of Thompson's groups $F_n$\n\nGili Golan\n\nJul 14 2026 math.GR arXiv:2607.10961v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2607.10961, author = {Gili Golan}, title = {{I}rreducible fast sets of bump homeomorphisms generate copies of {T}hompson's groups ${F}_n$}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2607.10961}, note = {arXiv:2607.10961v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nA homeomorphism of an interval is a positive bump if its support is a single open interval on which it moves every point to the right. Choosing a fundamental domain $[m,b(m))$ for the action of a positive bump $b$ on its support splits the remainder of the support into two intervals, called the feet of $b$. A finite set of positive bumps is geometrically fast if fundamental domains can be chosen so that all the resulting feet are pairwise disjoint. The crossing graph of such a set has the bumps as its vertices, two bumps being adjacent whenever their supports overlap but are not nested, and the set is irreducible if its crossing graph is connected. We prove that for every $n\\geq 2$, every group generated by an irreducible geometrically fast set of $n$ positive bumps is isomorphic to the $n$-ary Thompson group $F_n$. This answers the strong version of a problem posed by Brin and Zaremsky (Oberwolfach Rep. 15 (2018)).\n\n  * Higman--Thompson groups $F_n$ all the way down\n\nGili Golan\n\nJul 07 2026 math.GR arXiv:2607.04038v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2607.04038, author = {Gili Golan}, title = {{H}igman--{T}hompson groups ${F}_n$ all the way down}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2607.04038}, note = {arXiv:2607.04038v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n  * On Maximal Subgroups of Thompson's Group $F$\n\nGili Golan\n\nSep 08 2022 math.GR arXiv:2209.03244v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2209.03244, author = {Gili Golan}, title = {{O}n {M}aximal {S}ubgroups of {T}hompson's {G}roup ${F}$}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2209.03244}, note = {arXiv:2209.03244v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study subgroups of Thompson's group $F$ by means of an automaton associated with them. We prove that every maximal subgroup of $F$ of infinite index is closed, that is, it coincides with the subgroup of $F$ accepted by the automaton associated with it. It follows that every finitely generated maximal subgroup of $F$ is undistorted in $F$. We also prove that every finitely generated subgroup of $F$ is contained in a finitely generated maximal subgroup of $F$ and construct an infinite family of non-isomorphic maximal subgroups of infinite index in $F$.\n\n  * On closed subgroups of the R. Thompson group $F$\n\nGili Golan, Mark Sapir\n\nMay 04 2021 math.GR arXiv:2105.00531v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2105.00531, author = {Gili Golan and Mark Sapir}, title = {{O}n closed subgroups of the {R}. {T}hompson group ${F}$}, year = {2021}, eprint = {2105.00531}, note = {arXiv:2105.00531v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe prove that Thompson's group $F$ has a subgroup $H$ such that the conjugacy problem in $H$ is undecidable and the membership problem in $H$ is easily decidable. The subgroup $H$ of $F$ is a closed subgroup of $F$. That is, every function in $F$ which is a piecewise-$H$ function belongs to $H$. Other interesting examples of closed subgroups of $F$ include Jones' subgroups $\\overrightarrow{F}_n$ and Jones' $3$-colorable subgroup $\\mathcal F$. By a recent result of the first author, all maximal subgroups of $F$ of infinite index are closed. In this paper we prove that if $K\\leq F$ is finitely generated then the closure of $K$, i.e., the smallest closed subgroup of $F$ which contains $K$, is finitely generated. We also prove that all finitely generated closed subgroups of $F$ are undistorted in $F$. In particular, all finitely generated maximal subgroups of $F$ are undistorted in $F$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProblems · TheoremDB (https://theoremdb.org/problems/?collection=kourovka-notebook-problems)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; [#P3546] Quasi-isometry types involving Thompson’s group F Read statement Is Thompson’s group F quasi-isometric (a) to F × Z?\n[#P3550] Distributive lattices of right-relatively convex subgroupsRead statement Is it true that the lattice of rightrelatively convex subgroups of a right-orderable group is distributive if and only if it is a chain? This is Kourovka Notebook Problem $21.148$21.148.Directory date: 2026-08-12  | Group theory  | Open  | Submitted · 2mo\n[#P3548] Embedding groups of type F into higher finite